<header><h1>Production (Unit Ability): Proof</h1></header>
<article>
<p>The source text behind each note on <a href="/R_production">Production (Unit Ability)</a>. <b>Explicit</b> sources state the note directly. <b>Implicit</b> sources support it without stating it.</p>

<h2>Notes</h2>
    <ol class="note">
    <li>A unit&rsquo;s <sc>Production</sc> value is the maximum number of units that may be produced by that unit, regardless of cost. For example, a space dock with &ldquo;<sc>Production</sc> 6&rdquo; could produce six dreadnoughts or six fighters.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 68.1: &ldquo;This value is the maximum number of units that this unit can produce.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Does Production limit the number of units or their total cost?&rdquo;: &ldquo;The number of units &hellip; a war sun uses one point of Production and you pay its 12 cost separately.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Placement of produced units is not limited by a unit&rsquo;s <sc>Production</sc> value, only by the location of the producing units.<sup>1,2,5</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Where are produced ground forces placed?&rdquo;: &ldquo;With several producing planets you may split them among those planets however you like, even all on one.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>5</sup> Designer ruling (kaiteinomen, Sep 28, 2017): &ldquo;you can put both of the Letani warriors on the same planet, if you wish, as long as one of the units that used Production is located there.&rdquo; Only location of producing unit matters for placement.</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 68.3: &ldquo;When a player produces ground forces, that player must place those unit on planets that contain a unit that used its &lsquo;Production&rsquo; ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <ol><li>For example, say a player is performing a tactical action on tile 75. They have one <a href="/R_space_dock">Space Dock</a> I each on Abaddon and Ashtroth, but not on Loki. They combine their production to place seven infantry. They could place all seven on Abaddon, all seven on Ashtroth, or split them. They cannot place any on Loki.<sup>1,2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> Same entry: &ldquo;split them among those planets however you like.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>1</sup> LRR 68.3: &ldquo;When a player produces ground forces, that player must place those unit on planets that contain a unit that used its &lsquo;Production&rsquo; ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li></ol>
    <li>If an effect allows a player to produce a ship of a specific type, then factional variations do not prevent that player from producing that type of ship.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Do faction units like the Muaat Prototype War Sun count as their base unit type?&rdquo;: &ldquo;a faction unit counts as its base unit type for every purpose.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot produce ground forces on a planet that contains another player&rsquo;s ground forces.<sup>4</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>4</sup> Designer ruling: &ldquo;You can&rsquo;t produce ground forces on a planet with another player&rsquo;s ground forces.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <ol><li>A player whose units are <a href="/R_coexistence">coexisting</a> on a planet can produce ground forces on that planet; those ground forces also coexist.<sup>7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>9</sup> Designer Q&amp;A, &ldquo;Coexistence and PRODUCTION&rdquo;: &ldquo;Coexisting units can produce ground forces but are always blockaded and cannot produce ships.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>7</sup> Thunder&rsquo;s Edge Rulebook p. 11, Coexist: &ldquo;If the coexisting player places, produces, or moves additional units onto that planet, those units also coexist.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li></ol>
    <li>Ground forces produced during the <b>Production</b> step of a tactical action cannot be committed to land on a planet during that tactical action, as the <b>Invasion</b> step has already been resolved.<sup>2</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>2</sup> RulesPal, &ldquo;Can ground forces I produce take an uncontrolled planet in the same tactical action?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No &mdash; production is the last step, after invasion &hellip; They can invade in a later action.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    </ol>
</article>
